#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Written takeaways for Psalms 105-150."""

TAKEAWAYS = {
    # ---- Chapter 105 ----
    '105:1': "Thanksgiving is not kept indoors: the deeds are to be made known among people who have no part in them yet.",
    '105:2': "\"Talk ye of all his wondrous works.\" Singing and ordinary conversation are given the same subject matter.",
    '105:3': "Glorying is not forbidden, only relocated: the boast is moved off the self and onto his holy name.",
    '105:4': "\"Seek his face evermore.\" Having found him is not treated as the end of seeking him.",
    '105:5': "Remembering is commanded as work. Israel's faith is held together by a deliberate refusal to forget.",
    '105:6': "Seed of Abraham, children of Jacob — the congregation is told it belongs to the story about to be recited.",
    '105:7': "\"Our God\" and \"all the earth\" stand in one breath. The God of one people is not a local one.",
    '105:8': "The covenant holds because he remembered it, not because the thousand generations did.",
    '105:9': "Made with Abraham and sworn to Isaac: the promise is reissued to a son who did nothing to earn the reissue.",
    '105:10': "Confirmed to Jacob \"for a law\" — the promise hardens into something with the force of statute.",
    '105:11': "The land is called their inheritance before a foot of it is owned. The heir is named before the estate exists.",
    '105:12': "\"Very few, and strangers in it.\" The promise sat for generations with people who had no leverage at all.",
    '105:14': "\"He reproved kings for their sakes.\" The safety of the landless ran through the rebuke of the powerful.",
    '105:15': "\"Touch not mine anointed.\" Wandering herdsmen with no army are called anointed, and that is the whole defence.",
    '105:16': "\"He called for a famine.\" The hunger that drove Jacob's family to Egypt is described as God's own summons.",
    '105:17': "\"He sent a man before them, even Joseph, who was sold.\" The sending and the selling are one event read twice.",
    '105:18': "\"His feet they hurt with fetters: he was laid in iron.\" The psalm does not hurry past the prison years.",
    '105:19': "\"The word of the LORD tried him.\" The promise given to Joseph was also what tested him while it waited.",
    '105:20': "The king who held him is the one who looses him. Release comes through the hand that did the imprisoning.",
    '105:21': "A convict is made lord of the house. Egypt's second man is the Hebrew slave it had already condemned.",
    '105:23': "Jacob sojourned \"in the land of Ham\" — the rescue settles the family in the country that will enslave it.",
    '105:24': "The increase that alarmed Pharaoh is credited to God. Israel's growth under pressure was not an accident.",
    '105:25': "\"He turned their heart to hate his people.\" The psalm dares to put the persecution inside God's providence.",
    '105:26': "Moses his servant, Aaron his chosen. The deliverance arrives as two men rather than as an army.",
    '105:27': "The signs are worked in the land of Ham, on the ground where Egypt's own gods were supposed to be at home.",
    '105:28': "Darkness comes first, and the word that sent it met no resistance from the dark.",
    '105:29': "Water into blood: the river Egypt lived by is the first thing taken away from it.",
    '105:30': "Frogs reach \"the chambers of their kings.\" No room in Egypt was private enough to be spared.",
    '105:31': "\"He spake, and there came.\" The plagues arrive by a word, the same way the world they are unmaking did.",
    '105:32': "Hail instead of rain. The weather the fields depended on is what turns against them.",
    '105:33': "Vines and fig trees broken — the slow crops, the ones that take years to replace.",
    '105:34': "Locusts \"without number.\" The one plague the psalm refuses to count is the one nobody could.",
    '105:36': "\"The chief of all their strength.\" The firstborn are named as Egypt's power, and that is what is struck.",
    '105:37': "They leave with silver and gold and not one feeble person. Slaves walk out a paid and healthy nation.",
    '105:38': "\"Egypt was glad when they departed.\" The oppressor ends up relieved to lose what he would not release.",
    '105:39': "A cloud for covering and fire for light: one presence serves them differently by day and by night.",
    '105:40': "\"The people asked, and he brought quails.\" Here the wilderness grumbling is reported simply as a request answered.",
    '105:41': "The water from the rock runs \"like a river\" in dry places. The supply outlasts the moment of thirst.",
    '105:42': "The whole recital hangs on one clause: \"for he remembered his holy promise.\"",
    '105:43': "Joy and gladness, not relief, are the words chosen for a nation walking out of slavery.",
    '105:44': "They inherited \"the labour of the people.\" The gift arrives as somebody else's finished work.",
    '105:45': "The point of the land is obedience in it. The gift comes with a purpose attached and no price.",
}

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 106 ----
    '106:1': "\"He is good\" comes before \"his mercy endureth for ever.\" The character is stated before the duration.",
    '106:2': "A psalm about to recite Israel's failures opens by admitting that praise outruns anyone's power to speak it.",
    '106:3': "Blessedness is tied to doing righteousness \"at all times\" — which the next forty verses show nobody did.",
    '106:4': "\"Remember me\" — the singer asks to be let into a favour shown to a people, not to be paid as an individual.",
    '106:5': "Thy chosen, thy nation, thine inheritance. Everything he wants to enjoy belongs to somebody else first.",
    '106:6': "\"We have sinned with our fathers.\" The confession refuses the distance that would make this generation spectators.",
    '106:7': "The failure at the Red sea is traced to not remembering. Forgetting is the root sin in this telling.",
    '106:8': "\"Nevertheless he saved them for his name's sake.\" The reason given for the rescue is nothing found in them.",
    '106:9': "The sea is rebuked as though it were a person. The obstacle is addressed rather than negotiated with.",
    '106:10': "Saved and redeemed \"from the hand\" — the psalm keeps naming a grip, because a grip is what slavery was.",
    '106:11': "\"There was not one of them left.\" The water that made a road made a grave in the same hour.",
    '106:12': "\"Then believed they his words.\" Faith that waits until the sea has parted has a short half-life.",
    '106:13': "\"They soon forgat.\" The interval between the song and the forgetting is this psalm's real subject.",
    '106:14': "Appetite in the desert is called tempting God. Wanting is treated here as a theological act.",
    '106:15': "\"He gave them their request; but sent leanness into their soul.\" A granted prayer can itself be the judgement.",
    '106:16': "Envy of Moses is listed among the sins against God. Resentment of a leader is not a merely social fault.",
    '106:17': "The earth itself opens. The judgement on Dathan comes up from under the camp rather than out of it.",
    '106:18': "The fire is reported with no account of how it started. Judgement is recorded here, not explained.",
    '106:19': "A calf made in Horeb — at the mountain itself, while the covenant was still being given.",
    '106:20': "\"They changed their glory into the similitude of an ox that eateth grass.\" Idolatry is described as a bad trade.",
    '106:21': "\"They forgat God their saviour.\" The title is spoken at exactly the moment it was being denied.",
    '106:23': "Moses \"stood before him in the breach\" — the mediator is pictured as a man filling a gap in a broken wall.",
    '106:24': "\"They despised the pleasant land.\" Unbelief showed itself as contempt for a gift, not as doubt about a doctrine.",
    '106:25': "Murmuring in tents is counted as not hearkening. Private complaint is heard as public disobedience.",
    '106:26': "The sentence matches the sin: they did not want the land, and they will not see it.",
    '106:27': "The wilderness judgement is carried forward into the exile. One verse reaches across centuries of the same refusal.",
    '106:28': "\"The sacrifices of the dead.\" Baalpeor is described as a meal shared with nothing living.",
    '106:29': "\"Their inventions\" — the psalm's word for idolatry is a word for creativity.",
    '106:30': "Phinehas stands up and the plague stops. One man's act is given the weight Moses' intercession had.",
    '106:31': "\"Counted unto him for righteousness\" — the phrase used of Abraham's believing is used here of Phinehas' zeal.",
    '106:32': "Moses fared ill \"for their sakes.\" The people's provocation cost the one man who had stood in the breach for them.",
    '106:33': "\"He spake unadvisedly with his lips.\" The whole charge against Moses comes down to one sentence said in anger.",
    '106:34': "The sin named here is an omission: they simply did not do what they were told about the nations.",
    '106:35': "\"Were mingled among the heathen, and learned their works.\" Nobody decided to apostatise; they picked it up.",
    '106:36': "The idols they served became a snare. What was taken up as worship closed on them as a trap.",
    '106:37': "Child sacrifice is where the drift ends. The psalm traces the line from mingling all the way to this.",
    '106:38': "\"The land was polluted with blood.\" The ground is said to carry the guilt of what was done on it.",
    '106:39': "\"Defiled with their own works.\" The uncleanness was self-inflicted, not caught from the neighbours.",
    '106:40': "\"He abhorred his own inheritance.\" The psalm will not soften what God felt toward what belonged to him.",
    '106:41': "Ruled by those who hated them: the judgement is the exodus running backwards.",
    '106:42': "Oppression returns, and this time no Pharaoh is needed to explain it.",
    '106:43': "\"Many times did he deliver them.\" The rescues are counted, and they outnumber the lessons learned from them.",
    '106:44': "\"Nevertheless he regarded their affliction, when he heard their cry.\" A cry is not repentance, and it was enough.",
    '106:45': "He remembered \"his covenant\" and acted by the multitude of his mercies. The measure is his word, not their record.",
    '106:46': "Even the captors are made to pity them. Mercy arrives through people who owe them nothing.",
    '106:47': "The psalm ends still in exile, still asking. The history lesson closes as a prayer not yet answered.",
    '106:48': "The congregation is told to say Amen. This whole record of failure is meant to be agreed with out loud.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 107 ----
    '107:1': "The mercy thanked for is a covenant loyalty, which is why its length can be stated as a fact.",
    '107:2': "\"Let the redeemed of the LORD say so.\" Redemption is to be spoken by the redeemed, not reported about them.",
    '107:3': "Gathered from east, west, north and south: the congregation is collected out of scattering before it sings.",
    '107:4': "The first picture is not danger but aimlessness — wandering with no city to stop in.",
    '107:5': "Hunger and thirst are said to reach the soul. A body's lack is described as reaching the person.",
    '107:6': "\"Then they cried.\" The turn is not reform or sacrifice but noise made in trouble.",
    '107:7': "\"By the right way.\" The deliverance is not comfort where they were but a road that ends somewhere.",
    '107:8': "The refrain wishes rather than commands. \"Oh that men would\" concedes that mostly they do not.",
    '107:9': "The longing soul is satisfied and the hungry filled. The appetite is answered rather than rebuked.",
    '107:10': "Darkness, the shadow of death, affliction and iron. The second picture is prison, and it was earned.",
    '107:11': "The prisoners are told why they sit there. The psalm does not pretend the captives were innocent.",
    '107:12': "\"There was none to help.\" Being brought to the point of having nobody is itself described as God's work.",
    '107:13': "The guilty cry the same cry as the lost, and the answer comes back the same.",
    '107:14': "\"Brake their bands in sunder.\" The chains are not unlocked; they are broken.",
    '107:15': "The refrain returns unaltered after a stanza about rebels. The goodness praised is the same goodness.",
    '107:16': "Gates of brass, bars of iron — the psalm names the strongest thing it can think of and says it gave way.",
    '107:17': "\"Fools because of their transgression.\" Sin is diagnosed as stupidity before it is charged as guilt.",
    '107:18': "The sign given is loss of appetite. A man who will not eat is already at the gates of death.",
    '107:19': "This stanza cries in the present tense. The pattern is not being remembered; it is happening.",
    '107:20': "\"He sent his word, and healed them.\" The remedy is a word, sent, and that is the whole account of the cure.",
    '107:21': "Third refrain, third kind of trouble. The response asked for does not vary with the case.",
    '107:22': "Thanksgiving is given a form — sacrifice and telling. Gratitude here is something done, not only felt.",
    '107:23': "\"That do business in great waters.\" The fourth picture is ordinary work, not misadventure.",
    '107:24': "Those who go out on the sea \"see the works of the LORD.\" The dangerous place is also the place of sight.",
    '107:25': "The storm that nearly kills them is commanded. Weather is not outside the government of God here.",
    '107:26': "Up to heaven and down to the depths in one line: the psalm writes the motion of a ship into its grammar.",
    '107:27': "\"At their wit's end.\" Competent seamen are reduced to the condition of drunk men.",
    '107:28': "Professionals cry the same cry as the lost wanderers. Skill runs out at the same place ignorance does.",
    '107:29': "\"He maketh the storm a calm.\" What was raised by a word is stilled by one.",
    '107:30': "\"Glad because they be quiet.\" The relief comes at the silence, before the harbour is even reached.",
    '107:31': "The fourth refrain closes the four pictures. The psalm has been assembling a list of people who owe thanks.",
    '107:32': "The thanks is to be said among the people and before the elders. A private rescue is given a public setting.",
    '107:33': "The hand that made rivers makes wilderness. The reversal is shown running the wrong way first.",
    '107:34': "Fruitful land turned barren \"for the wickedness of them that dwell therein.\" Geography is read morally.",
    '107:35': "Wilderness into standing water: the sentence of two verses earlier is reversed word for word.",
    '107:36': "The hungry are settled where there was nothing and set to building. The gift includes the work.",
    '107:37': "Fields sown, vineyards planted. The rescue ends in ordinary agriculture and years of waiting for fruit.",
    '107:38': "Blessing is counted in people and cattle. The psalm is not embarrassed to measure it materially.",
    '107:39': "\"Again, they are minished and brought low.\" The psalm refuses to end the cycle at prosperity.",
    '107:40': "Princes wander where there is no way — put in the exact condition of the lost men in verse 4.",
    '107:41': "The poor are set on high and given families. The reversal is resettlement rather than revenge.",
    '107:42': "\"All iniquity shall stop her mouth.\" The argument ends not in defeat but in silence.",
    '107:43': "The last word goes to the wise: these things have to be observed before the lovingkindness is understood.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 108 ----
    '108:1': "\"My heart is fixed\" comes before the singing. The settledness is claimed, and the praise follows from it.",
    '108:2': "He wakes the instruments and then himself. The mood is not waited for; it is summoned.",
    '108:3': "Praise offered \"among the nations\" — the audience for Israel's worship is deliberately outside Israel.",
    '108:4': "Mercy above the heavens, truth to the clouds. The two are always measured together in this psalm.",
    '108:5': "The prayer asks for God's exaltation first and for anything of his own only afterwards.",
    '108:6': "\"That thy beloved may be delivered.\" The glory wanted in the line before is wanted for somebody's sake.",
    '108:7': "\"God hath spoken in his holiness.\" All the confidence about the map rests on a word already given.",
    '108:8': "Gilead, Manasseh, Ephraim, Judah — the territories are counted as already his because they were promised.",
    '108:9': "Moab a washpot, Edom a place to throw a shoe. The contempt is deliberate and not softened.",
    '108:10': "\"Who will bring me into the strong city?\" The confident inventory stops at the one place still unconquered.",
    '108:11': "He names God as the one who cast them off and in the same breath asks him to march out with the army.",
    '108:12': "\"Vain is the help of man.\" Not weak, not insufficient — vain. The alternative is cancelled rather than ranked.",
    '108:13': "\"Through God we shall do valiantly.\" The courage is theirs to show and his to supply.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 109 ----
    '109:1': "\"O God of my praise.\" He calls God by the title of his own song and then begs him not to stay silent.",
    '109:2': "The weapon complained of is a mouth. The whole assault described here is speech.",
    '109:3': "\"Without a cause.\" The hatred is not proportionate to anything done, because there was nothing to be proportionate to.",
    '109:4': "\"For my love they are my adversaries: but I give myself unto prayer.\" The answer to slander is not a reply.",
    '109:5': "Evil for good, hatred for love. The exchange rate is stated plainly before any curse is spoken.",
    '109:6': "A wicked judge and an accuser at his right hand: he asks that the man be given his own treatment back.",
    '109:7': "\"Let his prayer become sin.\" The hardest line in the psalm asks that even the man's religion be counted against him.",
    '109:8': "\"Let another take his office.\" Peter will read this line over the vacancy Judas left.",
    '109:9': "The curse reaches wife and children. The psalm does not pretend that hatred stops at the guilty man.",
    '109:10': "Begging children wandering from ruined homes. The wish is spelled out in detail rather than left general.",
    '109:11': "The extortioner is called in as the instrument. He asks that the man's own trade be turned against him.",
    '109:12': "\"Let there be none to extend mercy.\" What is asked for is the withdrawal of the one thing the psalmist himself lives on.",
    '109:13': "A blotted-out name was the worst loss Israel could imagine. The curse aims past the man at his future.",
    '109:14': "The father's iniquity remembered, the mother's sin not blotted out — the request runs backwards through a family.",
    '109:15': "The curses are set \"before the LORD continually\" and left there. He asks; he does not lift a hand.",
    '109:16': "The charge is finally named: he did not remember to shew mercy, and hunted men already broken in heart.",
    '109:17': "\"As he loved cursing, so let it come unto him.\" The punishment asked for is the man's own preference, delivered.",
    '109:18': "Cursing worn like a coat, then soaking in like water and oil. What was put on from outside works its way inward.",
    '109:19': "A garment and a girdle worn continually — he asks that the curse become clothing the man can never take off.",
    '109:20': "\"Let this be the reward... from the LORD.\" The whole terrible list is handed over rather than carried out.",
    '109:21': "\"But do thou for me... for thy name's sake.\" He asks for himself on entirely different ground than he asked against them.",
    '109:22': "\"I am poor and needy, and my heart is wounded within me.\" The hunter of the poor is answered by a poor man.",
    '109:23': "A shadow at evening, a locust shaken off. He describes himself as something already on its way out.",
    '109:24': "The fasting undertaken as prayer has left him unable to stand. Devotion is not described as making him stronger.",
    '109:25': "They shake their heads at him. The gospels record the same gesture in front of the cross.",
    '109:26': "\"According to thy mercy.\" The standard he wants applied to himself is not the one he asked for them.",
    '109:27': "The point of the rescue is what it will prove: \"that they may know that this is thy hand.\"",
    '109:28': "\"Let them curse, but bless thou.\" Two verdicts are passed on one man and only one of them holds.",
    '109:29': "Shame worn as a mantle answers cursing worn as a garment. The image is handed straight back.",
    '109:30': "Praise is promised \"among the multitude\" — in front of the same crowd that watched the shaming.",
    '109:31': "God stands at the right hand of the poor, which is the place the accuser was asked to take in verse 6.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 110 ----
    '110:1': "\"The LORD said unto my Lord.\" David calls his own descendant Lord, which is the question Jesus put to the Pharisees.",
    '110:2': "\"Rule thou in the midst of thine enemies.\" The reign is exercised while the opposition stands, not after it falls.",
    '110:3': "The people are willing \"in the day of thy power.\" The volunteers are produced by the power, not recruited before it.",
    '110:4': "Sworn and not repented of: a priesthood older than Aaron's and owing nothing to descent. Hebrews is built on this line.",
    '110:5': "The one seated at the right hand is also the one standing at it. The psalm refuses to keep its picture tidy.",
    '110:6': "Judgement among the heathen, described without euphemism. This king is not only a priest.",
    '110:7': "\"He shall drink of the brook in the way.\" The conqueror stops at a stream like any tired man on a march.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 111 ----
    '111:1': "Whole-hearted praise is located in the assembly. A private resolve is kept in public.",
    '111:2': "The works are \"sought out of all them that have pleasure therein.\" Delight is the condition of seeing anything.",
    '111:3': "His righteousness endures for ever is said of his work. What he does and what he is are not separable here.",
    '111:4': "\"He hath made his wonderful works to be remembered.\" The memorials were his idea, not Israel's good habit.",
    '111:5': "Food given and covenant remembered stand in one line. Daily provision is covenant keeping, not a separate kindness.",
    '111:6': "The power was shown for a purpose: to hand over an inheritance they could never have taken.",
    '111:7': "Works and commandments are called verity together. What he does and what he requires do not disagree.",
    '111:9': "\"He sent redemption unto his people\" — past tense and finished, before the name is called holy and reverend.",
    '111:10': "Wisdom begins in fear, and the good understanding belongs to those who do rather than those who know.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 112 ----
    '112:1': "He \"delighteth greatly in his commandments.\" Fear of God and delight in his law are put in one clause.",
    '112:2': "The blessing is described as landing on a generation the blessed man will not live to see.",
    '112:3': "\"His righteousness endureth for ever\" was said of God one psalm ago; here it is said of the man who fears him.",
    '112:4': "\"There ariseth light in the darkness.\" The upright are not promised the absence of dark, only light inside it.",
    '112:5': "Lending, and guiding his affairs with discretion. Goodness here looks like money handled well.",
    '112:6': "Everlasting remembrance is promised to a man whose name no chronicle is going to carry.",
    '112:7': "\"He shall not be afraid of evil tidings.\" The bad news is expected; what is promised is a fixed heart when it lands.",
    '112:8': "His heart is \"established\" — the steadiness is something done to him rather than mustered by him.",
    '112:9': "\"He hath dispersed, he hath given to the poor.\" Paul quotes this verse to describe a generosity that lasts.",
    '112:10': "The wicked man's grief is at seeing someone else blessed. Envy is given the last word about him.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 113 ----
    '113:1': "Servants are the ones told to praise. The call goes first to those with the least standing to make it.",
    '113:2': "\"From this time forth and for evermore.\" The praise is given a starting point and no ending one.",
    '113:3': "Sunrise to sunset. The praise is fixed to the daylight rather than to a temple or an hour of service.",
    '113:4': "High above the nations, glory above the heavens. The height is measured so the stooping can register.",
    '113:5': "\"Who is like unto the LORD our God?\" The question is asked about his height and answered by his descent.",
    '113:6': "He humbles himself even to look at heaven. The gap is such that noticing angels is condescension.",
    '113:7': "Out of the dust, off the dunghill. The lifting starts at the least dignified place the psalm can name.",
    '113:8': "\"That he may set him with princes.\" The beggar is not merely relieved but seated among the rulers.",
    '113:9': "The psalm's last picture of God's greatness is one barren woman with children in the house.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 114 ----
    '114:1': "\"A people of strange language.\" What is remembered of Egypt here is the experience of not being understood.",
    '114:2': "Judah his sanctuary, Israel his dominion. The nation itself is named as the place where God lives.",
    '114:3': "The sea fled and Jordan turned back. Two miracles forty years apart are set side by side as one motion.",
    '114:4': "Mountains skipping like rams: the psalm has the landscape behaving like a startled flock.",
    '114:5': "The psalm turns and interrogates the sea directly, as though demanding an explanation from it.",
    '114:6': "The question is put again to the hills, and the answer is withheld one verse longer than is comfortable.",
    '114:7': "The answer is not an explanation but a command. The presence itself was the reason all along.",
    '114:8': "Rock into water, flint into a fountain. The hardest thing the psalm can name is what is made to give drink.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 115 ----
    '115:1': "\"Not unto us, not unto us.\" The refusal of glory is said twice before the giving of it is said once.",
    '115:2': "The worry is the taunt \"Where is now their God?\" The reputation at stake is his rather than theirs.",
    '115:3': "\"He hath done whatsoever he hath pleased.\" The answer to the taunt is freedom, not a demonstration.",
    '115:4': "Silver and gold, \"the work of men's hands.\" The material is precious; the maker is the problem.",
    '115:5': "Mouths that do not speak, eyes that do not see. The mockery works by going through the organs one at a time.",
    '115:7': "Hands, feet, throat. The inventory ends with a body complete in every part and wholly inert.",
    '115:8': "\"They that make them are like unto them.\" A worshipper comes to resemble whatever he worships.",
    '115:9': "Israel is told to trust, and the ground given is not their record but his help and his shield.",
    '115:10': "The priests are given the same instruction as everyone else. The house of Aaron is not trusted to itself.",
    '115:11': "The third call reaches past Israel and past Aaron to anyone who fears him. The circle opens outward.",
    '115:12': "\"The LORD hath been mindful of us.\" What is expected is argued from what has already been received.",
    '115:13': "\"Both small and great.\" The blessing is explicitly denied a scale of importance.",
    '115:14': "The increase promised includes the children. Blessing is described as outliving the person blessed.",
    '115:15': "The blesser's credentials are the size of what he made. Heaven and earth stand behind the blessing.",
    '115:16': "Heaven kept, earth given away. Room for human work is made by God's own decision, not by his absence.",
    '115:17': "\"The dead praise not the LORD.\" The argument for wanting life is that praise needs a living voice.",
    '115:18': "\"But we will bless the LORD.\" Against the silence of the dead the congregation sets its own noise.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 116 ----
    '116:1': "\"I love the LORD, because he hath heard my voice.\" The love is given a reason, and the reason is an answered prayer.",
    '116:2': "Because the ear was inclined once, he will call for a lifetime. A single answer settles a habit.",
    '116:3': "Sorrows of death, pains of hell. The trouble took hold of him; he is not described as having walked into it.",
    '116:4': "The prayer in the crisis is one line long. Under the pains of hell nobody composes.",
    '116:5': "Gracious, righteous, merciful. The righteousness stands between the two mercies rather than against them.",
    '116:6': "\"The LORD preserveth the simple.\" Not the shrewd and not the strong, but those with no defences to speak of.",
    '116:7': "\"Return unto thy rest, O my soul.\" He has to argue his own soul back to a place it already had.",
    '116:8': "Soul, eyes, feet. The deliverance is itemised all the way down to not stumbling.",
    '116:9': "\"In the land of the living.\" The walking before God that he wants is in this life, not past it.",
    '116:10': "\"I believed, therefore have I spoken\" — and what he spoke was that he was greatly afflicted. Faith said the trouble out loud.",
    '116:11': "\"I said in my haste, All men are liars.\" The psalm preserves the sentence he now knows he said too fast.",
    '116:12': "\"What shall I render?\" The question assumes a debt, and every answer that follows is a form of receiving more.",
    '116:13': "The repayment offered is to take a cup. Gratitude here is discharged by accepting, not by giving back.",
    '116:14': "Vows paid \"in the presence of all his people.\" A rescue nobody saw is settled where everybody can.",
    '116:15': "\"Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints.\" Their dying is costly to him, which is why this one did not happen.",
    '116:16': "\"I am thy servant\" twice over, and son of a servant. He claims the household before mentioning the loosed bonds.",
    '116:17': "The sacrifice named is thanksgiving, and it comes with calling on the name again. Gratitude here keeps asking.",
    '116:18': "The vow is repeated word for word. That is how vows work: said once, and then said again where it counts.",
    '116:19': "The courts of the house, the midst of Jerusalem. The thanksgiving is pinned to an address before the psalm ends.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 117 ----
    '117:1': "The shortest psalm is addressed to the nations rather than to Israel. Paul quotes it to show the Gentiles were always intended.",
    '117:2': "The nations are told to praise God for a kindness shown \"toward us.\" They are called to rejoice at somebody else's mercy.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 118 ----
    '118:1': "The refrain stands at the head of the psalm, and everything that follows is offered as evidence for it.",
    '118:2': "Israel is told to say it. The congregation's part is to repeat the sentence, not to improve on it.",
    '118:3': "The priests are given the same words as the nation. Office adds nothing to what there is to say.",
    '118:4': "\"Them that fear the LORD\" — the last group named has no lineage and no office, only the fear.",
    '118:5': "Distress answered with \"a large place.\" The relief is described as room rather than as escape.",
    '118:6': "\"What can man do unto me?\" — asked by a man who is about to describe being surrounded by nations.",
    '118:7': "God \"taketh my part with them that help me.\" The allies are counted, and he is counted among them first.",
    '118:8': "Better to trust the LORD than man. It is put as a comparison, which means the other option was real.",
    '118:9': "The same sentence again with princes in it. Improving the quality of the man does not change the verdict.",
    '118:10': "\"All nations compassed me about.\" The odds are stated at their worst before the name of the LORD is brought in.",
    '118:11': "\"They compassed me about; yea, they compassed me about.\" The repetition is the pressure, written as pressure.",
    '118:12': "Bees, and a fire of thorns. Fierce, and over quickly — two images for the same enemy in one line.",
    '118:13': "\"Thou hast thrust sore at me.\" He turns and speaks to his attacker, then changes the subject to who caught him.",
    '118:14': "Strength, song and salvation. The line is lifted out of the song Israel sang at the Red sea.",
    '118:15': "The rejoicing is heard in tents rather than in a palace. Salvation reaches the ordinary houses first.",
    '118:16': "The right hand exalted, the right hand doing valiantly. It is said twice because a crowd is meant to answer.",
    '118:17': "\"I shall not die, but live, and declare the works of the LORD.\" The reason for wanting life is having something to say.",
    '118:18': "\"Chastened me sore: but he hath not given me over unto death.\" The chastening is owned, and so is its limit.",
    '118:19': "\"Open to me the gates of righteousness.\" He asks to be let in rather than claiming a right of entry.",
    '118:20': "The gate belongs to the LORD, and the righteous are simply those found going through it.",
    '118:21': "\"Thou art become my salvation.\" Deliverance turns out to be a person rather than an event.",
    '118:22': "\"The stone which the builders refused.\" The experts' rejection is what marks out the cornerstone; Jesus applied it to himself.",
    '118:23': "\"It is marvellous in our eyes.\" The reversal is not explained. It is stared at.",
    '118:24': "\"This is the day which the LORD hath made\" — the day meant is the day the rejected stone was set at the corner.",
    '118:25': "\"Save now, I beseech thee\" is Hosanna. The crowd at the gates of Jerusalem was shouting this verse.",
    '118:26': "\"Blessed be he that cometh in the name of the LORD.\" A welcome for pilgrims, said one day over one man.",
    '118:27': "\"Bind the sacrifice with cords, even unto the horns of the altar.\" The praise ends with an animal tied down.",
    '118:28': "\"Thou art my God\" twice. The possessive is the whole confession, and saying it again is the emphasis.",
    '118:29': "The psalm ends on the line it began with, unaltered by everything that came between.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 119: Aleph, Beth, Gimel, Daleth ----
    '119:1': "Blessedness is attached to the way walked rather than to the law known.",
    '119:2': "Keeping testimonies and seeking him with the whole heart are one activity. The law is treated as a road to a person.",
    '119:4': "\"Thou hast commanded us to keep thy precepts diligently.\" The standard is fixed before any of the pleading begins.",
    '119:5': "\"O that my ways were directed.\" The first thing he says about himself is a wish he cannot carry out.",
    '119:6': "Shame is escaped by respect unto \"all thy commandments.\" Partial keeping is precisely what leaves a man ashamed.",
    '119:8': "\"I will keep thy statutes: O forsake me not utterly.\" A resolution and a fear of abandonment in one breath.",
    '119:9': "How a young man cleanses his way: not by resolve but by taking heed to a word that came from outside him.",
    '119:10': "He has sought with his whole heart and still asks not to wander. Sincerity is not treated as a safeguard.",
    '119:11': "The word is hidden in the heart, not shelved. Sin is fought at the place where it is actually decided.",
    '119:12': "\"Blessed art thou, O LORD: teach me thy statutes.\" Praise and a request for instruction come out as one sentence.",
    '119:13': "\"With my lips have I declared.\" What was hidden in the heart two verses ago is now said out loud.",
    '119:14': "The testimonies are rejoiced in \"as much as in all riches\" — measured against wealth, not against other laws.",
    '119:17': "\"That I may live, and keep thy word.\" Life is asked for as the condition of obedience, not as its reward.",
    '119:18': "\"Open thou mine eyes.\" The wondrous things are already in the law; what needs fixing is the reader.",
    '119:19': "\"I am a stranger in the earth.\" A man with standing nowhere asks not to be kept from the one thing he has.",
    '119:20': "His soul breaks with longing, and what he longs for is judgments. The appetite is aimed at something austere.",
    '119:23': "Princes sit and speak against him; he sits and meditates. Two kinds of sitting in a single verse.",
    '119:24': "The testimonies are called \"my counsellors.\" The word he must obey is also the word he goes to for advice.",
    '119:25': "\"My soul cleaveth unto the dust\" — and what he asks for is not relief but quickening according to the word.",
    '119:26': "\"I have declared my ways, and thou heardest me.\" Confession comes first and the request to be taught after it.",
    '119:27': "Understanding is wanted so that he can talk of it. The learning is aimed at speech, not at private possession.",
    '119:28': "\"My soul melteth for heaviness.\" Strength is asked for according to the word, because that is where he has learned to look.",
    '119:29': "\"Grant me thy law graciously.\" The law itself is requested as a gift rather than taken up as a duty.",
    '119:32': "\"I will run the way of thy commandments, when thou shalt enlarge my heart.\" The pace waits on something done to him.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 119: He, Vau, Zain, Cheth ----
    '119:33': "He asks to be taught the way and expects to keep it \"unto the end.\" The teaching is what makes persistence possible.",
    '119:34': "Understanding is asked for as the thing that produces obedience. He does not expect to keep what he cannot grasp.",
    '119:36': "\"Incline my heart unto thy testimonies, and not to covetousness.\" The rival is named outright, and it is money.",
    '119:37': "The prayer is about what the eyes rest on. Vanity is treated as something caught by looking at it.",
    '119:38': "\"Stablish thy word unto thy servant.\" He asks God to make good on what God has already said.",
    '119:39': "He admits the reproach he fears, and the reason he gives is not his innocence but that the judgments are good.",
    '119:41': "Mercies and salvation are asked \"according to thy word.\" He wants only what has already been promised.",
    '119:42': "His answer to the man who reproaches him is a word he trusts and did not compose.",
    '119:43': "\"Take not the word of truth utterly out of my mouth.\" He dreads being left with nothing true to say.",
    '119:45': "\"I will walk at liberty: for I seek thy precepts.\" The freedom is the result of the seeking, not its opposite.",
    '119:46': "Speaking of the testimonies \"before kings.\" What he says in private is what he expects to say at court.",
    '119:48': "\"My hands also will I lift up unto thy commandments.\" The gesture of worship is made toward the law itself.",
    '119:49': "\"Upon which thou hast caused me to hope.\" Even the hoping is traced back to God's doing rather than his own temper.",
    '119:50': "\"This is my comfort in my affliction.\" The word is not what removes the trouble but what he lives on inside it.",
    '119:51': "Derision from the proud did not move him. The pressure named here is contempt, not violence.",
    '119:53': "\"Horror hath taken hold upon me\" over other men's forsaking of the law. The distress is not about himself.",
    '119:54': "\"My songs in the house of my pilgrimage.\" Statutes set to music by a man who is not home yet.",
    '119:56': "\"This I had, because I kept thy precepts.\" A rare line where he names something obedience actually gained him.",
    '119:57': "\"Thou art my portion.\" The word for a Levite's inheritance is claimed by a man with no land at all.",
    '119:59': "\"I thought on my ways, and turned my feet.\" Thinking is reported as having changed the direction he walks.",
    '119:60': "\"I made haste, and delayed not.\" Speed is counted here as part of the obedience rather than a detail of it.",
    '119:62': "Thanksgiving at midnight. The hour chosen is the one with nobody awake to see it.",
    '119:63': "His company is chosen by what other men fear. Nothing else about them is mentioned.",
    '119:64': "\"The earth is full of thy mercy: teach me thy statutes.\" Out of the whole creation he asks for one lesson.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 119: Teth, Jod, Caph, Lamed ----
    '119:65': "\"Thou hast dealt well with thy servant.\" The verdict on his life is given before the affliction is brought up.",
    '119:67': "\"Before I was afflicted I went astray.\" He credits the trouble with the correction without pretending he chose it.",
    '119:68': "\"Thou art good, and doest good.\" What God is and what God does are stated as one thing before the request follows.",
    '119:69': "A lie is forged against him and his answer is to keep the precepts. The slander is not returned in kind.",
    '119:70': "\"Their heart is as fat as grease.\" What he diagnoses in the proud is insensibility rather than hostility.",
    '119:71': "\"It is good for me that I have been afflicted.\" Said flatly, as a conclusion reached rather than a sentiment offered.",
    '119:72': "The law of his mouth set against thousands of gold and silver — a comparison made in a currency he does not have.",
    '119:73': "\"Thy hands have made me\" is the argument for \"give me understanding.\" The maker is asked to finish his own work.",
    '119:74': "His hoping is expected to gladden other people. One man's trust turns out to be public property.",
    '119:75': "\"Thou in faithfulness hast afflicted me.\" The trouble is not excused as an accident but credited to God's faithfulness.",
    '119:78': "They dealt perversely \"without a cause,\" and his whole response is to go on meditating. Nothing is planned against them.",
    '119:80': "A sound heart in the statutes is what keeps a man from shame, and the soundness is asked for, not claimed.",
    '119:81': "\"My soul fainteth for thy salvation: but I hope in thy word.\" Hope is stated as fact while the strength is gone.",
    '119:82': "\"When wilt thou comfort me?\" The waiting has lasted long enough to be complained of to the one waited for.",
    '119:83': "\"Like a bottle in the smoke\" — a wineskin shrivelled over the fire. He describes himself as dried out and past use.",
    '119:84': "\"How many are the days of thy servant?\" He points out that he has not got unlimited time to wait in.",
    '119:85': "The pits dug for him are called \"not after thy law.\" The offence he names first is against the law, not against him.",
    '119:86': "\"All thy commandments are faithful: they persecute me wrongfully.\" Two facts set side by side and left unreconciled.",
    '119:88': "Quickening is asked according to lovingkindness, and the reason given is so that he can go on keeping the testimony.",
    '119:89': "\"For ever, O LORD, thy word is settled in heaven.\" Its stability is located where his circumstances cannot reach it.",
    '119:90': "Faithfulness to all generations is proved by the ground underfoot. The earth is produced as evidence.",
    '119:91': "\"All are thy servants.\" Sun and season are described as obeying in the way he is still trying to.",
    '119:92': "\"I should then have perished in mine affliction.\" He says plainly that delight in the law is what kept him alive.",
    '119:93': "The precepts are never to be forgotten because \"with them thou hast quickened me.\" Gratitude is the reason, not discipline.",
    '119:94': "\"I am thine, save me.\" The plea rests on belonging; the seeking is only mentioned second.",
    '119:95': "The wicked wait for him and he considers the testimonies. Those two activities are the whole of his situation.",
    '119:96': "\"An end of all perfection: but thy commandment is exceeding broad.\" Everything else has a limit he has already reached.",
})
